  <h1>How Defamation Damages Work</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Actual damages</h2><p>The most concrete category: documented economic harm from the defamatory statement. Lost employment or contracts, reduced business revenue, medical bills for emotional distress treatment. These are provable and carry the most weight with juries.</p>
  <h2>General / presumed damages</h2><p>In per se defamation cases, the plaintiff may recover for general harm to reputation and emotional distress without proving specific economic loss. Juries award a lump sum for reputational harm based on the severity and reach of the false statement. After <em>Gertz v. Robert Welch, Inc.</em> (1974), states may only allow presumed damages for private figures; public figures must prove actual malice to recover any damages.</p>
  <h2>Punitive damages</h2><p>Available only when actual malice is established: the defendant knew the statement was false or acted with reckless disregard. Punitive damages punish egregious conduct and deter future misconduct. The Supreme Court has not imposed a specific cap on defamation punitive damages, but lower courts often apply a ratio of 1&ndash;4&times; compensatory damages under due process principles.</p>
  <h2>The public figure problem</h2><p><em>New York Times Co. v. Sullivan</em> (1964) and <em>Gertz v. Robert Welch, Inc.</em> (1974) established that public figures (both all-purpose and limited-purpose) must prove actual malice to recover any compensatory damages. This First Amendment protection for speech about public figures severely limits recovery unless the defendant knew the statement was false.</p>
